 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428147 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428148 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Separation of Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1033 1179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1034 1.517971234312146 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "701ed6eb-4bd0-5344-bf15-d9a52b59f3af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113682 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113682 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113682 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a41b96bf-c760-5d9e-965d-35966e8ead16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113683 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dQ}{dt} - \\frac{8}{150 - t}Q = 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113683 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113683 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8044bd9-9c69-5038-8c52-fc8f726d9531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113684 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{7}{150 - t}Q = 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113684 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113684 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dd707408-7202-575f-b59c-f13ea66be4e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113685 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{8}{150 - t}Q = 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113685 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113685 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "295374a5-dc24-56a6-a0a2-7633dece9fa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113686 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dQ}{dt} - \\frac{7}{150 - t}Q = 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113686 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113686 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c6eb109-acf9-58b5-bb33-fbdff3675f46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113687 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{1}{150 - t}Q = 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113687 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113687 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7019544e-7b04-559e-aa53-e74900a49d04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113688 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113688 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113688 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c7762cd8-8692-5ad4-968f-3c3e39a3e1f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114961 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114961 #edb/ref 1071 "A vat initially holds $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of a sugar solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar.\nA sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar per liter is poured into the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ What differential equation describes the amount of sugar $Q,$ in kilograms, in the vat $t$ minutes after the inflow begins?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8438bb87-bee8-594a-98a6-4c0117ba64c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114961 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that a sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =5.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of solution in the vat at time $t.$\n\n- The vat initially contains $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Sugar solution enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the vat.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $30t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the vat.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n360 + 18t - 30t = (360-12t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =5.4-\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{60-2t}Q=5.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114962 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114962 #edb/ref 1071 "A reactor initially holds $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of a chemical solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved reactant.\nPure solvent is pumped into the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed chemical solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent pumping begins." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4777bc9-c65f-5da2-b962-96b38daa7518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114962 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nNote that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of chemical solution; solvent enters at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and drains out at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of chemical solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n300 + 14t - 14t = 300\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{300}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{lb}}{\\textrm{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed chemical solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7Q}{150}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{7Q}{150}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{7}{150}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{7}{150} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{7}{150})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{7}{150}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{7t}{150}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{7t}{150}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-7t/150},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 18,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-7\\cdot 0/150} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 18e^{-7t/150}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(25) = 18e^{-175/150} \\approx 5.61\\,\\textrm{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114963 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114963 #edb/ref 1071 "A $12\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$-capacity tank initially holds $8\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ of water containing $320\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7aa89a9d-8785-50a9-a517-201f7514136c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114963 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\textrm{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $8\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 8\\,000 + 100t - 60t = 40(200+t)\\,\\textrm{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{40(200 + t)}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{40(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{40(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{2(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3Q}{2(200+t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{3}{2(200 + t)}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{3}{2(200+t)} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{2(200+t)})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-3/2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-3/2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{3/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $320\\,\\textrm{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 320,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =320 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{3/2}} & =320 \\\\ A & =320(200)^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{320(200)^{3/2}}{(200+t)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =320{(\\frac{200}{200+t})}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $12\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 12\\,000$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =12\\,000 \\\\ 40(200+{t}_{o}) & =12\\,000 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =320{(\\frac{200}{200+100})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =320{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3/2} \\\\  & ≈174.19\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing a Dilution Model" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186114961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115373 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f279c6af-692d-54f6-9e78-cab02b95d186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115374 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374823 #edb/ref 175921