#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b958274c-e6c0-599a-87d9-822a2901b003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115790 #edb/ref 1071 "A tank initially holds $450L$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $9kg$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $21L/min$, while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $24L/min$. Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water begins to be added?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01e441d3-5537-5224-9166-58a5232542e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115791 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115791 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115791 #edb/ref 1071 "A tank initially holds $120gal$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $20lb$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $4gal/min$, while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $4gal/min$.\nFind an equation for the amount of salt $Q(t)$, in pounds, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water is first added.$Q(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115791 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64193270-c02d-55f7-b1b5-b71007015ee2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115791 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116336 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116336 #edb/ref 1061 593289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116336 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca6cb0e8-4c90-5c6d-bf08-803296752852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116336 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116337 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116337 #edb/ref 1061 593290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116337 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d55c798d-8387-51fe-9d00-6c58dd6d6dc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116337 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116338 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116338 #edb/ref 1061 593291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116338 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "251dbebe-a8e1-57f6-b8c1-54c43e9332c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116338 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116339 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116339 #edb/ref 1061 593294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116339 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "28ce70f4-759b-530e-a692-0d92aa4ffb8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116339 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116340 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116340 #edb/ref 1061 593295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116340 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d7e1fc1-8a54-5f2f-85f5-542931de3855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116340 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116341 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116341 #edb/ref 1061 593299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116341 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "259727bc-a70f-53ae-9206-f6024bda6e30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116341 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116342 #edb/ref 1061 593300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116342 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5981b624-3f7f-52e8-a1c9-8c3bd471d2b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116342 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116425 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a container which initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance.\n\n- Another solution, containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of the substance per gallon, flows *into* the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$\n- At the same time, the completely mixed solution flows *out* of the container at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/31/312d11bb0a00f6f668b653b00a7eecda55cdb23c5a7c09076734774b7c05a50b.png)\n\nHow can we model the amount $Q$ (in pounds) of substance in the container at time $t?$\n\nTo model $Q(t),$ we can analyze how it changes over time. The rate of change of the amount of substance in the container is given by $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out},$$ where:\n\n- $Q'_\\text{in}$ is the rate at which the substance *enters* the container. A solution containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ of the substance enters the container at a rate of $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$ So, we have $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'} & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =be\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n- $Q'_\\text{out}$ is the rate at which the substance *leaves* the container. To determine the rate of exit, we first find the volume of solution in the container at time $t.$\n\nStarting from the initial volume $V_0,$ we add the volume that enters, $et,$ and subtract the volume that leaves, $ft.$ Thus, the volume of solution at time $t$ is $$(V_0 + et - ft)\\,\\textrm{gal}.$$ Since $Q$ is the mass of substance in the container, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is $$\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}.$$ So, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ the substance leaves the container at the rate of $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} & =(f\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\ & =(f\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\ & =f(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft})\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = be - f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) = be}$$\n\nLet's see this in a more concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116425 #edb/ref 1065 14562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116425 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "be8f798d-68e4-549b-a2c6-ce3fd435c044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116425 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116426 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant. A solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nLet's find a differential equation that describes the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$$ represents the rate at which this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank:\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n- Solution enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the tank.\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is $$450 + 8t - 8t = 450\\,\\textrm{L}.$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is $$\\dfrac{Q}{450}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\ & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\ & =\\frac{8Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\ & =\\frac{4Q}{225}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} \\\\ & =0-\\frac{4Q}{225}\\end{aligned}$$ which finally gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{225}Q=0.\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116426 #edb/ref 1065 14563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116426 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b84bba1-b479-506a-8e25-77650b293eee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116426 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116427 #edb/ref 1064 "Previously, we saw that the volume $V$ of solution at time $t$ in a container with initial volume $V_0,$ input rate $e,$ and output rate $f$ is given by\n\n$$V(t) = V_0 + (e - f)t.$$\n\nThere are three possible behaviors for the volume depending on the rates:\n\n- The volume *remains constant* when the input rate equals the output rate, $e=f.$ In this case, $$V(t) = V_0.$$\n- The volume *decreases* when the output rate is greater than the input rate, $f > e > 0,$ and the container begins to empty. Let $t_e$ be the time when the container empties: $V(t_e) = 0.$ Solving for $t_e,$ we get $$\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{e}) & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e} \\\\ 0 & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e}.\\end{aligned}$$ Solving this equation, we find that the container empties at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_e = \\dfrac{V_0}{f - e}}.$\n\n- The volume *increases* when the input rate is greater than the output rate, $e > f > 0,$ and the container begins filling until it reaches its capacity $V_\\text{cap}.$ Let $t_o$ be the time when the container reaches capacity: $V(t_o) = V_\\text{cap}.$ Solving for $t_o,$ we get $$\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & ={V}_{\\text{cap}} \\\\ {V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{o} & ={V}_{\\text{cap}}.\\end{aligned}$$ Solving this equation, we find that the container overflows at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_o = \\dfrac{V_\\text{cap} - V_0}{e - f}}.$\n\nNow, let's apply one of these formulas in a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116427 #edb/ref 1065 14564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116427 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3c551e08-3014-52f3-83f1-73ec27aa2e82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116427 #edb/ref 1067 "Points of Overflow and Emptying" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116428 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we have seen that if a container initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance, and\n\n- a solution with concentration $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ flows into the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ while\n- the completely mixed solution flows out at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min},$\n\nthen the amount $Q(t)$ of the substance in the container satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = be.$$\n\nFor the rest of this lesson, we'll focus on the special case where the input solution contains *no substance*, that is, where $b=0.$ In this case, the right-hand side vanishes, and the differential equation becomes *separable*:\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac1Q\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = - \\dfrac{f}{V_0 + (e-f)t}$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is negative, meaning the amount of substance in the container decreases over time. We can now solve the differential equation directly by separation of variables.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider our previous example: a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, and a solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nWe found that the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank after $t$ minutes is modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{4}{225}Q = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{4}{225}.$$\n\nWe solve this equation by integrating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}\\,dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{225})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\,dQ & =-\\frac{4}{225}\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|Q| & =-\\frac{4t}{225}+C \\\\ Q & ={e}^{-4t/225+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-4t/225}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNote that $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nFinally, since the tank initially contains $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-4(0)/225} & =6 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{Q(t) = 6e^{-4t/225}}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116428 #edb/ref 1065 14654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116428 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a4e43cc-21fe-5b6e-ab72-10aef2431693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116428 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving a Dilution Model Where the Input Has a Concentration of Zero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374820 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374820 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374820 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374820 #edb/ref 1071 "A tank initially holds $260\\,\\text{L}$ of a dye solution containing $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye.\nPure water is poured into the tank at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the pure water is first added.\n\n$Q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374820 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc17f053-4154-5b58-a673-a3b7c9595b38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374820 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye) enters the tank at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (13\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (13\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of solution in the tank remains $260\\,\\text{L}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(13\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(13\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{20} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{20})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{20}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/20+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/20},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=18{e}^{-t/20}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374821 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374821 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374821 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374821 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "17c7259c-bd73-5489-8857-2aa304112761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374822 #edb/ref 1080 "18{e}^{-t/20}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374822 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374822 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "835ed941-71af-508b-b962-5bfd810b7918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374826 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374826 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374826 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374826 #edb/ref 1071 "A tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of salt $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water is first added." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3c2a5b4-3b5e-50f6-abee-7b39dd34294c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374826 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $12t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+18t-12t=(600+6t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{2Q}{100+t} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{100+t}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{100+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{100+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{100+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(100+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(100+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ A & =25\\cdot {100}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=25{(\\frac{100}{100+t})}^{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374827 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374827 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374827 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374827 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374827 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e5507560-e644-5b06-9ffc-b163d1f6589b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374828 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t)=20{(\\frac{100}{100+t})}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "587ad7eb-e7ff-5449-a3e7-315421a5c0c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374829 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t)=2500{(\\frac{10}{100+t})}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374829 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374829 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "95d74708-bfdc-53f7-9971-439077d9c125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374830 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t)=25\\frac{100}{(100+t)^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374830 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374830 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c1ca561d-2102-5596-b774-af92e2184dde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374831 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t)=25{(\\frac{100}{100+t})}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28f84566-0022-5a52-aa79-a865a20593a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374832 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t)=20{(\\frac{10}{100+t})}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374832 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374832 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "64fc0482-ab86-5439-a2a9-8a2e97fe0fb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374840 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374840 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374840 #edb/ref 1071 "A wastewater holding basin initially holds $480\\,\\text{gal}$ of water containing $12\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminants.\nClean water is pumped into the basin at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid leaves the basin at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminants in the basin $10$ minutes after the clean water pumping begins." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374840 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7733334-ec35-5062-9a3d-109d06df1cd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374840 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminants in the basin at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminants enter the basin minus the rate at which contaminants leave the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants enter the basin.\n\nNote that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminants) enters the basin at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminants* enter the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants exit the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminants leave the basin, we first determine the volume of wastewater in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $480\\,\\text{gal}$ of wastewater; water enters at $24\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of wastewater in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n480+24t-18t=(480+6t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminants in the basin, the *concentration* of the wastewater at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminants* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{3}{80+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{3}{80+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{80+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{80+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(80+t)^{3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $12\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminants. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=12,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(80+0)^{3}} & =12 \\\\ A & =12\\cdot {80}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12{(\\frac{80}{80+t})}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminants in the basin $10$ minutes after the clean water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(12)=12{(\\frac{80}{90})}^{3}≈8.43\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374841 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374841 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374841 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374841 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf9b1d72-7dcf-5756-8025-f1f5167127d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374842 #edb/ref 1080 "8.43\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374842 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374842 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22446d9b-f3b3-51d7-8eab-f813f126f966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374843 #edb/ref 1080 "7.54\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374843 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374843 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fc791120-4af1-5df5-8a8b-8cf249933b52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374844 #edb/ref 1080 "8.22\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374844 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374844 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a979bc1-8055-5284-be10-31b92bc2fb71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374845 #edb/ref 1080 "8.08\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374845 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374845 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b2ab7b77-90c7-560a-a520-39ae98136e1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374846 #edb/ref 1080 "8.70\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374846 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374846 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5fb6f7cb-44c0-5462-90c0-5e816014480d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336727" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374854 #edb/ref 1071 "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity reactor initially holds $7\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of a chemical solution containing $98\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. Pure solvent begins to flow into the reactor at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed solution flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45ce4d33-a7f8-5fd0-bbf2-c53f0966bb82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374854 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $7\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,500+90t-60t=30(250+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{250+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{250+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{250+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{250+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{250+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{250+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|250+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(250+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(250+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(250+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $98\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=98,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =98 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(250+0)^{2}} & =98 \\\\ A & =98(250)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{98(250)^{2}}{(250+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =98{(\\frac{250}{250+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(250+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 250+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =98{(\\frac{250}{250+100})}^{2} \\\\  & =98{(\\frac{250}{350})}^{2} \\\\  & =98{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =50\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374855 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374855 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374855 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374855 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "86cb9083-a221-51ed-ad2c-ae6d13e72d9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374856 #edb/ref 1080 "48\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374856 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374856 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03d90ed3-593b-56eb-b15c-74fb96a264c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374857 #edb/ref 1080 "50\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374857 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374857 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "23a41576-4255-58aa-aff3-24970b0750f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374858 #edb/ref 1080 "55\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374858 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374858 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "162b2841-c921-5383-b411-667946024eb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374859 #edb/ref 1080 "60\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374859 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374859 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a400a2ae-64d3-5df6-8b72-04dc312a8573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374860 #edb/ref 1080 "52\\,\