in}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $190\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed solution leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=190+6t-4t=2(95+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{95+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{95+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{95+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{190+2t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{190+2t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{95+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|95+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|95+t|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(95+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(95+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(95+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=32,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(95+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ A & =32(95)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{32(190)^{2}}{(95+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =32{(\\frac{95}{95+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $310\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=310$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =310 \\\\ 190+2{t}_{o} & =310 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =32{(\\frac{95}{95+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =32{(\\frac{95}{155})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈12.02\\,\\text{lb},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374862 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374862 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374862 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374862 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "617c7a09-689e-563c-b4f9-daf6d3871ab7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374863 #edb/ref 1080 "11.60\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374863 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374863 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7cb4e1cb-a4d9-5a79-8bad-83176cb4a640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374864 #edb/ref 1080 "11.85\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374864 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374864 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "97b47761-bf18-5c2c-807b-5fbd6eecd397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374865 #edb/ref 1080 "12.46\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374865 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374865 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f9316b8e-3e20-5a4c-8d57-cf7e0b5d688e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374866 #edb/ref 1080 "12.19\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374866 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374866 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8dde20d6-6676-5b10-94da-9eee8eb3183f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374867 #edb/ref 1080 "12.02\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374867 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374867 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07f0a2a1-f819-5785-94c7-449359e5a659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374868 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374868 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374868 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374868 #edb/ref 1071 "A $9\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity vat initially holds $6\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of a sugar-water solution containing $300\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. Pure water begins to flow into the vat at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed sugar-water flows out at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374868 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98bdf48f-bd8c-581b-9efb-c84bc03b4bb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374868 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of sugar) enters the vat at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of sugar-water in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $6\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure water enters at $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the vat after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=6\\,500+100t-80t=20(325+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the vat is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(80\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(80\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{325+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{325+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{4}{325+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{325+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{325+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{325+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|325+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(325+t)^{-4}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(325+t)^{-4}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(325+t)^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $300\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=300,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =300 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(325+0)^{4}} & =300 \\\\ A & =300(325)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{300(325)^{4}}{(325+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =300{(\\frac{325}{325+t})}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the vat begins to overflow. The vat has a capacity of $9\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,500 \\\\ 20(325+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,500 \\\\ 325+{t}_{o} & =475 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =150\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(150) & =300{(\\frac{325}{325+150})}^{4} \\\\  & =300{(\\frac{13}{19})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈65.75\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374869 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374869 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374869 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374869 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8cfba8ca-3fd1-51fd-9b05-5695d3defec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374870 #edb/ref 1080 "67.49\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374870 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374870 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "89eecff6-8175-550d-ad43-2b9766bd7fa6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374871 #edb/ref 1080 "65.75\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374871 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374871 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ed6a48d-1428-52d9-a522-2bee31663016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374872 #edb/ref 1080 "63.98\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374872 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045